vertical asymptote at \\(x = \\frac{3}{2}\\).\n\nThus, \\(b = 1\\).\n\n### Step 4: Identify Horizontal Asymptotes\nTo find horizontal asymptotes, we compare the degrees of the numerator and the denominator.\n\nThe degree of the numerator \\(x^2 - x + 1\\) is 2.\nThe degree of the denominator \\(6x - 9\\) is 1.\n\n","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,37175,279,4771,315,26107,59,37018,45340,61,18,6558,61,17,37892,15170,21,87,61,17,12,24,87,92,76466,10061,400,64,3,387,279,1372,315,19766,304,279,4771,11,400,65,3,387,279,1372,315,12140,438,88,1307,6295,11,400,66,3,387,279,1372,315,16202,96254,6295,11,323,400,67,3,387,279,1372,315,1508,36426,96254,6295,13,7379,400,64,10,17,65,10,18,66,10,19,67,12947,151645,198,151644,77091,198,1249,11625,279,3491,11,582,1184,311,23643,279,729,1124,11520,37018,45340,61,18,481,856,61,17,488,856,15170,21,87,61,17,481,220,24,87,11035,8,3019,553,3019,382,14374,14822,220,16,25,61242,1437,279,5712,198,5338,11,8168,279,63533,323,279,46912,382,785,63533,374,510,78045,856,61,18,481,856,61,17,488,856,284,856,2075,61,17,481,856,488,220,16,8,1124,2533,785,46912,374,510,78045,220,21,87,61,17,481,220,24,87,284,220,18,87,7,17,87,481,220,18,8,1124,2533,4416,11,279,729,9044,510,78045,1124,37018,45340,2075,61,17,481,856,488,220,16,9139,90,18,87,7,17,87,481,220,18,9139,1124,2533,1654,646,9121,279,4185,8168,17767,87,57758,320,65363,17767,87,1124,811,80,220,15,59,9957,78045,1124,37018,45340,61,17,481,856,488,220,16,15170,18,7,17,87,481,220,18,9139,1124,2533,14374,14822,220,17,25,64547,472,7151,198,32,14178,304,279,4771,13666,1380,2176,279,63533,323,279,46912,614,264,4185,8168,429,646,387,33446,13,758,419,1142,11,1052,525,902,4185,9363,1948,279,63533,17767,87,61,17,481,856,488,220,16,57758,323,279,46912,17767,18,7,17,87,481,220,18,10699,568,15277,11,1052,525,902,19766,382,44500,11,17767,64,284,220,15,59,3593,14374,14822,220,18,25,64547,35463,1634,1600,417,6295,198,18612,96254,6295,12170,1380,279,46912,374,7168,323,279,63533,374,2477,36929,13,2573,279,46912,6144,311,7168,323,11625,369,17767,87,59,982,78045,220,18,7,17,87,481,220,18,8,284,220,15,1124,921,78045,220,17,87,481,220,18,284,220,15,1124,921,78045,856,284,1124,37018,90,18,15170,17,92,1124,2533,4416,11,1052,374,825,12140,96254,1272,518,17767,87,284,1124,37018,90,18,15170,17,11035,3593,44500,11,17767,65,284,220,16,59,3593,14374,14822,220,19,25,64547,39909,1634,1600,417,6295,198,1249,1477,16202,96254,6295,11,582,9429,279,12348,315,279,63533,323,279,46912,382,785,8381,315,279,63533,17767,87,61,17,481,856,488,220,16,57758,374,220,17,624,785,8381,315,279,46912,17767,21,87,481,220,24,57758,374,220,16,382],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nConsider the graph of $$\\frac{x^3-x^2+x}{6x^2-9x}.$$Let $a$ be the number of holes in the graph, $b$ be the number of vertical asympotes, $c$ be the number of horizontal asymptotes, and $d$ be the number of oblique asymptotes. Find $a+2b+3c+4d$.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"b2849f1830b6fd76e0da604f9f90c48c1494705790d920422baaa52dd80a8afd"},"seconds":41.66508984565735,"seed":456522445789314046,"stop":"eos","text":"Since the degree of the numerator is greater than the degree of the denominator, there is no horizontal asymptote.\n\nThus, \\(c = 0\\).\n\n### Step 5: Identify Oblique Asymptotes\nOblique asymptotes occur when the degree of the numerator is exactly one more than the degree of the denominator. In this case, the degree of the numerator is 2 and the degree of the denominator is 1, so there is an oblique asymptote.\n\nTo find the equation of the oblique asymptote, perform polynomial long division of \\(x^2 - x + 1\\) by \\(2x - 3\\):\n\n1. Divide the leading term of the numerator by the leading term of the denominator:\n   \\[ \\frac{x^2}{2x} = \\frac{1}{2}x \\]\n\n2. Multiply \\(\\frac{1}{2}x\\) by the entire divisor \\(2x - 3\\):\n   \\[ \\frac{1}{2}x \\cdot (2x - 3) = x^2 - \\frac{3}{2}x \\]\n\n3. Subtract this from the original numerator:\n   \\[ (x^2 - x + 1) - (x^2 - \\frac{3}{2}x) = -x + \\frac{3}{2}x + 1 = \\frac{1}{2}x + 1 \\]\n\n4. Repeat the process with the new polynomial \\(\\frac{1}{2}x + 1\\):\n   \\[ \\frac{\\frac{1}{2}x}{2x} = \\frac{1}{4} \\]\n\n5. Multiply \\(\\frac{1}{4}\\) by the entire divisor \\(2x - 3\\):\n   \\[ \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot (2x - 3) = \\frac{1}{2}x - \\frac{3}{4} \\]\n\n6. Subtract this from the new polynomial:\n   \\[ (\\frac{1}{2}x + 1) - (\\frac{1}{2}x - \\frac{3}{4}) = 1 + \\frac{3}{4} = \\frac{7}{4} \\]\n\nThus, the quotient is \\(\\frac{1}{2}x + \\frac{1}{4}\\), and the oblique asymptote is:\n\\[ y = \\frac{1}{2}x + \\frac{1}{4} \\]\n\nSo, there is one oblique asymptote.\n\nThus, \\(d = 1\\).\n\n### Step 6: Calculate the Final Expression\nNow, we calculate \\(a + 2b + 3c + 4d\\):\n\\[ a + 2b + 3c + 4d = 0 + 2(1) + 3(0) + 4(1) = 0 + 2 + 0 + 4 = 6 \\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{6}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":true},"graded_text":"To solve the problem, we need to analyze the function \\(\\frac{x^3 - x^2 + x}{6x^2 - 9x}\\) step by step.\n\n### Step 1: Simplify the Function\nFirst, factor the numerator and the denominator.\n\nThe numerator is:\n\\[ x^3 - x^2 + x = x(x^2 - x + 1) \\]\n\nThe denominator is:\n\\[ 6x^2 - 9x = 3x(2x - 3) \\]\n\nSo, the function becomes:\n\\[ \\frac{x(x^2 - x + 1)}{3x(2x - 3)} \\]\n\nWe can cancel the common factor \\(x\\) (assuming \\(x \\neq 0\\)):\n\\[ \\frac{x^2 - x + 1}{3(2x - 3)} \\]\n\n### Step 2: Identify Holes\nA hole in the graph occurs where both the numerator and the denominator have a common factor that can be canceled. In this case, there are no common factors between the numerator \\(x^2 - x + 1\\) and the denominator \\(3(2x - 3)\\). Therefore, there are no holes.\n\nThus, \\(a = 0\\).\n\n### Step 3: Identify Vertical Asymptotes\nVertical asymptotes occur where the denominator is zero and the numerator is non-zero. Set the denominator equal to zero and solve for \\(x\\):\n\\[ 3(2x - 3) = 0 \\]\n\\[ 2x - 3 = 0 \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{3}{2} \\]\n\nSo, there is one vertical asymptote at \\(x = \\frac{3}{2}\\).\n\nThus, \\(b = 1\\).\n\n### Step 4: Identify Horizontal Asymptotes\nTo find horizontal asymptotes, we compare the degrees of the numerator and the denominator.\n\nThe degree of the numerator \\(x^2 - x + 1\\) is 2.\nThe degree of the denominator \\(6x - 9\\) is 1.\n\nSince the degree of the numerator is greater than the degree of the denominator, there is no horizontal asymptote.\n\nThus, \\(c = 0\\).\n\n### Step 5: Identify Oblique Asymptotes\nOblique asymptotes occur when the degree of the numerator is exactly one more than the degree of the denominator. In this case, the degree of the numerator is 2 and the degree of the denominator is 1, so there is an oblique asymptote.\n\nTo find the equation of the oblique asymptote, perform polynomial long division of \\(x^2 - x + 1\\) by \\(2x - 3\\):\n\n1. Divide the leading term of the numerator by the leading term of the denominator:\n   \\[ \\frac{x^2}{2x} = \\frac{1}{2}x \\]\n\n2. Multiply \\(\\frac{1}{2}x\\) by the entire divisor \\(2x - 3\\):\n   \\[ \\frac{1}{2}x \\cdot (2x - 3) = x^2 - \\frac{3}{2}x \\]\n\n3. Subtract this from the original numerator:\n   \\[ (x^2 - x + 1) - (x^2 - \\frac{3}{2}x) = -x + \\frac{3}{2}x + 1 = \\frac{1}{2}x + 1 \\]\n\n4. Repeat the process with the new polynomial \\(\\frac{1}{2}x + 1\\):\n   \\[ \\frac{\\frac{1}{2}x}{2x} = \\frac{1}{4} \\]\n\n5. Multiply \\(\\frac{1}{4}\\) by the entire divisor \\(2x - 3\\):\n   \\[ \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot (2x - 3) = \\frac{1}{2}x - \\frac{3}{4} \\]\n\n6. Subtract this from the new polynomial:\n   \\[ (\\frac{1}{2}x + 1) - (\\frac{1}{2}x - \\frac{3}{4}) = 1 + \\frac{3}{4} = \\frac{7}{4} \\]\n\nThus, the quotient is \\(\\frac{1}{2}x + \\frac{1}{4}\\), and the oblique asymptote is:\n\\[ y = \\frac{1}{2}x + \\frac{1}{4} \\]\n\nSo, there is one oblique asymptote.\n\nThus, \\(d = 1\\).\n\n### Step 6: Calculate the Final Expression\nNow, we calculate \\(a + 2b + 3c + 4d\\):\n\\[ a + 2b + 3c + 4d = 0 + 2(1) + 3(0) + 4(1) = 0 + 2 + 0 + 4 = 6 \\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]","id":"intermediate_algebra:881"}
